From 2695e217981f3522150e2ab985b712663ae02866 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: work-ple <work-ple@flashnext.local>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 23:09:53 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 10/12] [ROCm] Wire VLLM_PLE_MMAP and the FP8 PLE stack into
 the AMD tree

Before this, on a ROCm build VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1 was a silent no-op: the amd/
tree never imported ple_mmap, the custom op was never registered, and the
stock GPU-resident VocabParallelEmbedding was built as if the flag were
unset. Mirror the five nvidia wiring sites into amd/, plus the FP8 embedding
stack the amd tree never had.

amd/ple_layer.py
  W1  'from typing import cast'.
  W2  'from ..common import ple_mmap' (module-scope, so the op registers
      at import; RocmPlatform.dispatch_key == "CUDA", so registration and
      the CPU-key guard behave exactly as on CUDA).
  W3  Qwen4ExpNGramEmbedding.__init__ constructor branch:
      ple_mmap.enabled() -> check_cudagraph_safety + validate_shards_for +
      MmapNgramEmbedding(padded_vocab_size, head_dim); else the stock path.
      The branch logs loudly: it takes the ROCm tree OFF the FULL-cudagraph
      mode its short-conv path is written for, and off the AMD gather op.
      VLLM_PLE_MMAP=0 (the default) is the kill switch.
  W4  forward() mmap branch, placed BEFORE the AMD gather op: that op
      allocates its output at ngram_embedding.params_dtype, which
      MmapNgramEmbedding does not have, and it is not in
      CompilationConfig._attention_ops, so it is not a split boundary and
      would run inside the capture. The trigram hashing is factored out of
      forward into _hash_ngram_ids -- byte-identical body, and the NAME is
      load-bearing: ple_mmap's op body probes for it to decide whether
      layer_name resolves to a PLE layer.
  W5a/W5b  load_weights interceptions: ngram_embedding.weight_scale ->
      ple_mmap.set_weight_scale (the placeholder has no such Parameter for
      AutoWeightsLoader to find); shard tensors dropped with
      weights_streamed = True. 'embedding' hoisted out of the shard branch,
      the stock reads cast to VocabParallelEmbedding.

Blocker A -- the FP8 PLE stack, ported at full parity from nvidia/:
  Qwen4ExpPLEFp8EmbeddingMethod, _get_ple_embedding_quant_method,
  Qwen4ExpPLELayer._get_embedding_weight_scale / _dequantize_embeddings,
  the quant_config=/params_dtype= plumbing through
  Qwen4ExpNGramEmbedding.__init__ and its caller, and the
  _dequantize_embeddings call in the PLE forward before key_proj/value_proj.
  Without it an FP8 checkpoint's ngram_embedding.weight_scale is an orphan
  name the AMD loader hands to AutoWeightsLoader (ValueError), and
  copy_ple_embedding_shard_ bare-casts FP8 -> bf16, doubling the bytes and
  discarding the block scale.

  One AMD-only addition the nvidia file does not need:
  _embedding_output_dtype(). The AMD gather op writes into a preallocated
  buffer; allocating it at params_dtype (bf16) would make output.copy_() a
  bare FP8 -> BF16 cast that drops the scale before _dequantize_embeddings
  ever sees an FP8 tensor. It returns the embedding's real weight dtype,
  which equals params_dtype for an unquantized table.

  fp8 stays torch.float8_e4m3fn throughout (create_fp8_weight_parameter
  hardcodes it, and RocmPlatform.is_fp8_fnuz() is "gfx94" only, so gfx1151
  is not fnuz). The checkpoint bytes are OCP e4m3 on every platform;
  relabelling them fnuz would be numerically wrong.

amd/model.py: get_current_vllm_config import, the ple_mmap import, and the
two 5-line build_tables blocks after load_weights. amd/model.py is now
byte-identical to nvidia/model.py (diff is empty).

tests/models/qwen4_exp/test_ple_mmap_amd.py: new, no GPU and no ROCm
runtime required. Pins that both trees reach the same ple_mmap module
object, that _hash_ngram_ids still carries the name the op probes for, the
env-on/env-off constructor swap, the two cudagraph guards firing from the
AMD constructor, the load_weights scale-keep/shard-drop, the FP8 method
selection, the output-buffer dtype, and dequant (including the fail-closed
raise when an FP8 table has no scale).

No nvidia-side behavior changes.

